  "categories": ["linked-list"],
  "descriptionMd": "Given the `head` of a singly linked list, turn the list around so that its last node comes first and its first node comes last. Return the head of the reversed list.\n\n**Example 1**\n\n```\nhead = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n→ [5, 4, 3, 2, 1]\n```\n\n**Example 2**\n\n```\nhead = [1, 2]\n→ [2, 1]\n```\n\n**Follow-up:** once you have one approach, try writing it the other way — iterative vs. recursive.",
  "constraints": ["0 ≤ number of nodes ≤ 5000", "-5000 ≤ Node.val ≤ 5000"],
  "hints": ["Walk the list once and flip each node's `next` pointer as you go.", "Before you redirect a pointer, make sure you still know how to reach the rest of the list."],
